#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481389 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-2x)}{y-2x}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y-2x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le y-2x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y-2x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-2x)}{y-2x}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{u}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{u}\\,|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{u}\\,du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡u}{2}]}_{1}^{4}dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({ln}^{2}⁡4-{ln}^{2}⁡1)\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{ln}^{2}⁡4{\\int }_{0}^{2}dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2}\\cdot [v]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & ={ln}^{2}⁡2\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =2{ln}^{2}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481405 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨x+\\sqrt{y},\\hspace{0.222em}y+\\sqrt{x}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and the line $y=0$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/e5/e570374b37c116e32c3a7762b8cf09a28bdbb3e06b5e67e25837902a89bcd264.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x+\\sqrt{y}$ and $Q(x,y)=y+\\sqrt{x}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}y\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}x\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481410 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-xy)\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(3,2),$ $(5,4),$ $(5,2),$ and $(3,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}-xy=x(x-y)\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=x-y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(3,2) & =(3,3-2) \\\\  & =(3,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(5,4) & =(5,5-4) \\\\  & =(5,1) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(5,2) & =(5,5-2) \\\\  & =(5,3) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(3,0) & =(3,3-0) \\\\  & =(3,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,3\\le u\\le 5,\\,1\\le v\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/e0/e0b810c03e7e3210fcff3a739e3781ad5ff694bfe44d5f73d7782c3b4108a286.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-1\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}x(x-y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}uv\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}uv\\,|-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{3}^{5}uv\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}v\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{3}^{5}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}]}_{1}^{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{3}^{5} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481418 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}y{cos}^{2}⁡(y-x)\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,|y-x|\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le y-x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y-x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le u\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y{cos}^{2}⁡(y-x)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}v{cos}^{2}⁡u\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}v{cos}^{2}⁡u\\cdot |-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}v[{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡u\\,du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}v[{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2u+1)du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}v[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2u}{2}+u]_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\pi v\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}[\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481423 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+2y)\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(1,-\\frac{1}{2}),$ $(\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{3}{4}),$ $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{\\pi -3}{4}),$ and $(1,\\frac{\\pi -2}{4}).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+2y)\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=2x,\\,v=x+2y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(1,-\\frac{1}{2}) & =(2\\cdot 1,1+2\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =(2,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{3}{4}) & =(2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3}{2}+2\\left(-\\frac{3}{4}\\right)) \\\\  & =(3,0) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{\\pi -3}{4}) & =(2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3}{2}+2\\left(\\frac{\\pi -3}{4}\\right)) \\\\  & =(3,\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(1,\\frac{\\pi -2}{4}) & =(2\\cdot 1,1+2\\left(\\frac{\\pi -2}{4}\\right)) \\\\  & =(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 3,\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/dd/dd628fd99b4684d79e8c3b0f4e6696f72582abaef18eef729f5611711a2e42ec.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+2y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,|\\frac{1}{4}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{2}^{3}u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{2}^{3}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot [\\operatorname{sin}⁡v]_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{2}^{3}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 1\\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481424 :question/problem "Consider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le 2y-x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y-x\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables $u=2y-x$ and $v=y-x,$ the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u$ and $v$ as" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2y-x,\\,v=y-x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nIn this case, we first need to solve the transformation equations\n\n$$\nu=2y-x,\\,v=y-x\n$$\n\n \nfor $x$ and $y$, which gives\n\n$$\nx=u-2v,\\,y=u-v.\n$$\n\n \n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}{(u-2v)}^{2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{(u-2v)}^{2}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{(u-2v)}^{2}\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481429 :question/problem "Consider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,\\,1-(x+1)^{2}\\le y\\le 2-(x+1)^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables $u=x+1$ and $v=y+(x+1)^{2},$ the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(x-y+1)\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u$ and $v$ as" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+1,\\,v=y+(x+1)^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows::\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,1\\le v\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2(x+1) & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nIn this case, we first need to solve the transformation equations\n\n$$\nu=x+1,\\,v=y+(x+1)^{2}\n$$\n\n \nfor $x$ and $y$, which gives\n\n$$\nx=u-1,\\,y=v-{u}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x-y+1)\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}[(u-1)-(v-{u}^{2})+1]|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}({u}^{2}+u-v)\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}({u}^{2}+u-v)\\cdot |1|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}+u-v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481430 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(2{x}^{2}-xy-{y}^{2})\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(1,-1),$ $(2,0),$ $(3,-2),$ and $(2,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2{x}^{2}-xy-{y}^{2}=(2x+y)(x-y)\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\\,v=x-y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}(1,-1) & =(2(1)+(-1),1-(-1)) \\\\  & =(1,2) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,0) & =(2(2)+0,2-0) \\\\  & =(4,2) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(3,-2) & =(2(3)+(-2),3-(-2)) \\\\  & =(4,5) \\\\ {T}^{-1}(2,-3) & =(2(2)+(-3),2-(-3)) \\\\  & =(1,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,2\\le v\\le 5\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/9f/9f5030bc3437fbc4431dd5350620bc7f9133d2bf910654607c0c5f7ef292f3a3.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2{x}^{2}-xy-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}(2x+y)(x-y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}uv\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}uv\\,|-\\frac{1}{3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{5}{\\int }_{1}^{4}uv\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{5}v\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{1}^{4}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}]}_{2}^{5}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{21}{2}\\cdot \\frac{15}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{105}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481431 :question/problem "Using the change of variables $u=x+y$ and $v=x-y,$ evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(x+y)(x-y)^{3/2}\\,dx\\,dy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x+y\\le 3,\\,0\\le x-y\\le 1\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=x-y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }-1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x+y)(x-y)^{3/2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}u{v}^{3/2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{3}u{v}^{3/2}|-\\frac{1}{2}|du\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{v}^{3/2}[{\\int }_{1}^{3}u\\,du]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{v}^{3/2}{[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{3}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{v}^{3/2}[\\frac{9}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}]dv \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{v}^{3/2}\\,dv \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{2}{5}\\,{v}^{5/2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}[1-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481432 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+2xy+{y}^{2}){e}^{x-y}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x+y\\le 3,\\,0\\le x-y\\le 2\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le x+y\\le 3,\\,0\\le x-y\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+y\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x-y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }\\text{ }1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+2xy+{y}^{2}){e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}(x+y)^{2}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{u}^{2}{e}^{v}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{u}^{2}{e}^{v}|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{u}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{v}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{u}^{2}[{e}^{v}]_{0}^{2}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{u}^{2}\\cdot ({e}^{2}-1)\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{2}{[\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{26}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{13({e}^{2}-1)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481434 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y-x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le x+y\\le \\pi \\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n0\\le y-x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le x+y\\le \\pi ,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y-x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x+y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le v\\le \\pi \\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ \\text{ }\\text{ }\\text{ }1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot [-\\operatorname{cos}⁡v]_{0}^{\\pi }\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot 2\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,du \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{sin}⁡u]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481440 :question/problem "Using the change of variables $u=x+y$ and $v=x-y,$ evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,dx\\,dy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x+y\\le 2,\\,2\\le x-y\\le 4\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=x-y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,2\\le v\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }-1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}(x+y)(x-y)|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}uv\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{∬}_{Δ}uv\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u[{\\int }_{2}^{4}v\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u{[\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}]}_{2}^{4}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u(\\frac{{4}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2})\\,du \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2}u\\,du \\\\  & =3[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482511 :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,\\pi \\le x+y\\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le y+z\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nGiven the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y+z,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the missing part of the expression below?\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+z)\\,dV={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAA}}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$ \n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y+z,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps: \n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,\\pi \\le u\\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le v\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le w\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+z)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot |1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482515 :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le \\frac{y}{x}\\le 2,\\,1\\le xy\\le 3,\\,0\\le z\\le xy+3\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{x},\\,v=xy,\\,w=z-xy,\n$$\n\nthe triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}xyz\\,dV\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w$ as" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{x},\\,v=xy,\\,w=z-xy,\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 3,\\,-v\\le w\\le 3\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x} & 0 \\\\ y & x & 0 \\\\ -y & -x & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ y & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{x}-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\  & =-2u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}xyz\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}v(w+v)\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{2u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{v(w+v)}{2u}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{-v}^{3}\\frac{v(w+v)}{2u}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482530 :question/problem "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y+z,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+z)\\,dV,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,\\pi \\le x+y\\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le y+z\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le 1\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y+z,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,\\pi \\le u\\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le v\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le w\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+z)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\cdot |-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}dw \\\\  & =[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u]_{u=\\pi }^{u=2\\pi }\\cdot {[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡v]}_{v=0}^{v=\\pi }\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=1} \\\\  & =(-1+(-1))\\cdot (-(-1)+1)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482534 :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le xy\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=xy,\\,w=3z,\n$$\n\n \n\nthe triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}({x}^{2}y+3xyz)\\,dV\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w$ as" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=xy,\\,w=3z,\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2,\\,0\\le w\\le 3\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ y & x & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ y & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(x-0) \\\\  & =3x \\\\  & =3u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}({x}^{2}y+3xyz)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}(uv+vw)\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}(uv+vw)\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3u}|\\,du\\,dv\\,dw \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\,\\frac{1}{3}(v+\\frac{vw}{u})\\,\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482540 :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,2\\le x-y\\le 3,\\,1\\le {x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\le 4,\\,1\\le z\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x-y,\\,v={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,w=3z,\n$$\n\nthe triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}6z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)\\,dV\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w$ as" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x-y,\\,v={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,w=3z,\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le 4,\\,3\\le w\\le 6\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2x & -2y & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2x & -2y\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(-2y+2x) \\\\  & =6(x-y) \\\\  & =6u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{6u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}6z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}2w\\operatorname{ln}⁡u\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{6u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{w\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{3u}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{w\\operatorname{ln}⁡u}{3u}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482547 :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 3,\\,-2\\le x-z\\le 0,\\,1\\le \\frac{z-1}{y}\\le 5\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n1\\le y\\le 3,\\,-2\\le x-z\\le 0,\\,1\\le \\frac{z-1}{y}\\le 5,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=y,\\,v=x-z,\\,w=\\frac{z-1}{y}.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,-2\\le v\\le 0,\\,1\\le w\\le 5\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{z-1}{{y}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=u.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |u|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{1}^{5}u\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}u\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{-2}^{0}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{5}dw \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]}_{u=1}^{u=3}\\cdot [v]_{v=-2}^{v=0}\\cdot [w]_{w=1}^{w=5} \\\\  & =(\\frac{9}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (0-(-2))\\cdot (5-1) \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482580 :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)\\le 2\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=y,\\,w=z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1).\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2,\\,0\\le w\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{2y}{{y}^{2}+1} & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=0}^{u=2}\\cdot [v]_{v=0}^{v=2}\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=2} \\\\  & =(2-0)\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482599 :question/problem "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y+z,\\,v=x+4z,\\,w=2y\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\,\\frac{y+z}{x+4z}\\,dV,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y+z\\le 2,\\,1\\le x+4z\\le e,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=y+z,\\,v=x+4z,\\,w=2y\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le e,\\,0\\le w\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(-1)^{3+2}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(-1)(-1). \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{y+z}{x+4z}\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\cdot |\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{2v}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{u}{2v}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{u}{2}\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{v}\\,dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}dw \\\\  & =[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{4}]_{u=0}^{u=2}\\cdot {[\\operatorname{ln}⁡v]}_{v=1}^{v=e}\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=2} \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482606 :question/problem "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+{y}^{2},\\,v=y+z,\\,w=z\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}(x+{y}^{2})^{9}z\\,dV,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x+{y}^{2}\\le 1,\\,0\\le y+z\\le 5,\\,0\\le z\\le 4\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+{y}^{2},\\,v=y+z,\\,w=z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 5,\\,0\\le w\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2y & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(x+{y}^{2})^{9}z\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}{u}^{9}w\\cdot |1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}{u}^{9}w\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{u}^{9}w\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{9}\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{5}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{4}w\\,dw \\\\  & =[\\frac{{u}^{10}}{10}]_{u=0}^{u=1}\\cdot {[v]}_{v=0}^{v=5}\\cdot [\\frac{{w}^{2}}{2}]_{w=0}^{w=4} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{10}-0)\\cdot (5-0)\\cdot (8-0) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482608 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve ${y}^{2}=1-{x}^{2}+2y.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =1-{x}^{2}+2y \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=1.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,2)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(-1,2)={f}_{y}(-1,2)i-{f}_{x}(-1,2)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(-1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y)j \\\\  & =(2y-2)i-2x\\,j \\\\ t(-1,2) & =(2(2)-2)i-2(-1)\\,j \\\\  & =2\\,i+2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $2\\,i+2\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482615 :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y+3z\\le 2,\\,1\\le {z}^{3}\\le 8\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y+3z\\le 2,\\,1\\le {z}^{3}\\le 8,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=y+3z,\\,w={z}^{3}.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 2,\\,1\\le w\\le 8\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3{z}^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3{z}^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (3{z}^{2})-3\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =3{z}^{2} \\\\  & =3{w}^{2/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3{w}^{2/3}}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3{w}^{2/3}}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{8}{w}^{-2/3}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{8}{w}^{-2/3}dw \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[u]_{u=0}^{u=2}\\cdot [v]_{v=0}^{v=2}\\cdot [3{w}^{1/3}]_{w=1}^{w=8} \\\\  & =(2-0)\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot ({8}^{1/3}-{1}^{1/3}) \\\\  & =(2-0)\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482616 :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le {y}^{2}-x\\le 2,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,1\\le z\\le e\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n1\\le {y}^{2}-x\\le 2,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,1\\le z\\le e,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu={y}^{2}-x,\\,v=y,\\,w=z.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,-1\\le v\\le 1,\\,1\\le w\\le e\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2y & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{e}1\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}du\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{1}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{e}dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=1}^{u=2}\\cdot [v]_{v=-1}^{v=1}\\cdot [w]_{w=1}^{w=e} \\\\  & =(2-1)\\cdot (1-(-1))\\cdot (e-1) \\\\  & =2(e-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483356 :question/problem "The inequality $-3<x<-1$ can be expressed in interval notation as" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses $()$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets $[]$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur inequality contains only \"less than\" symbols. So, the endpoints $-3$ and $-1$ are *not* included. \n\n$$\n-3\\,\\,<\\,\\,x\\,\\,<\\,\\,-1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n(-3,-1)\n$$\n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in (-3,-1).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483360 :question/problem "![](images/2b/2b64c156519b685acf390c51c1ea3e1540d82e0f7e047cf2f804d38a23956604.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $\\mathbf{F}?$\n\nI. $\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=0$\n\nII. $\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}>0$\n\nIII. $\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}<0$\n\nIV. $\\mathbf{F}$ is solenoidal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ at a point $P$ measures how much $\\mathbf{F}$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $\\mathbf{F}$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $\\mathbf{F}$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $\\mathbf{F}$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=0$ everywhere in the domain of $\\mathbf{F},$ we say that $\\mathbf{F}$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statements I and III are false. Notice that the length of the vectors' $x$-components increase as we move away from the origin in the horizontal direction. Therefore, the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ acts like a source at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}\\ne 0,$ the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483377 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $$\n\\mathbf{F}={x}^{2}yz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{x}^{3}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+z\\operatorname{arctan}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\n$$ Evaluate $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(-1,-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+R\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}yz)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z\\operatorname{arctan}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)) \\\\  & =2xyz+{x}^{3}+\\operatorname{arctan}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(-1,-1,2),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}(-1,-1,2) & =2(-1)(-1)(2)+(-1)^{3}+\\operatorname{arctan}\\left(\\frac{-1}{-1}\\right) \\\\  & =4-1+\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =3+\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483380 :question/problem "The interval $y\\in [-1,2)$ corresponds to the inequality" :question/worked-solution "Every interval can be expressed as an inequality. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses $()$ to indicate that the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets $[]$ to indicate that the endpoints *are* included.\n\nFor the given interval \n\n$$\n[-1,2\\,)\n$$\n\nwe note the following:\n\n- The left endpoint $-1$ *is* included in the interval. Therefore, we use $\\le \\,.$\n\n- The right endpoint $2$ is *not* included in the interval. Therefore, we use $<\\,.$\n\nTherefore, the inequality that corresponds to the given interval is \n\n$$\n-1\\,\\,\\le \\,\\,y\\,\\,<\\,\\,2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483383 :question/problem "The inequality $-5\\le x<-2$ can be expressed in interval notation as" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses $()$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets $[]$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nFor the given inequality \n\n$$\n-5\\,\\,\\le \\,\\,x\\,\\,<\\,\\,-2\n$$\n\nwe note the following:\n\n- The left endpoint $-5$ *is* included in the interval. Therefore, we use $[\\,.$\n\n- The right endpoint $-2$ is *not* included in the interval. Therefore, we use $)\\,.$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding interval is\n\n$$\nx\\in [-5,-2).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483384 :question/problem "Which inequality corresponds to the interval $x\\in [-7,0]?$" :question/worked-solution "Every interval can be expressed as an inequality. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses $(,)$ to indicate that the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets $[]$ to indicate that the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur interval contains only brackets. So, the endpoints *are* included. \n\n$$\nx\\in [-7,0]\n$$\n\nTherefore, our interval can be expressed as the following inequality:\n\n$$\n-7\\,\\,\\le \\,\\,x\\,\\,\\le \\,\\,0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483395 :question/problem "![](images/c1/c1dd3e659d2580103232fa68620c60f493477649e1c4a8880023e6c6de88a167.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $\\mathbf{F}?$\n\nI. $\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=0$\n\nII. $\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}>0$\n\nIII. $\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}<0$\n\nIV. $\\mathbf{F}$ is solenoidal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ at a point $P$ measures how much $\\mathbf{F}$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $\\mathbf{F}$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $\\mathbf{F}$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $\\mathbf{F}$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=0$ everywhere in the domain of $\\mathbf{F},$ we say that $\\mathbf{F}$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement III is true, whereas statements I and II are false. Notice that the lengths of the vector's $y$-components decrease as we move vertically towards the origin. Therefore, the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ acts like a sink at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}\\ne 0,$ the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483404 :question/problem "![](images/05/05b54d1b8e401f49861e335fd4736566441017576b30d9b56a6bdfe5e670bffb.png)\n\nWhich of the following intervals corresponds to the segment shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses $()$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets $[]$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nThe given interval can be represented by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n-3\\,\\,<\\,\\,x\\,\\,<\\,\\,2\n$$\n\nWe can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\in (-3,2)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483411 :question/problem "The inequality $-\\sqrt{2}<x<\\sqrt{2}$ can be expressed in interval notation as" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses $()$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets $[]$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur inequality contains only \"less than\" symbols. So, the endpoints $-\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\sqrt{2}$ are *not* included. \n\n$$\n-\\sqrt{2}\\,\\,<\\,\\,x\\,\\,<\\,\\,\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})\n$$\n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483422 :question/problem "![](images/e9/e91f27df78e4d7c05477917f0875add047f24fa2a2aee2ea26d6191023e80165.png)\n\nWhich of the following intervals corresponds to the segment shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses $()$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets $[]$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nThe given interval can be represented by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\,\\le \\,\\,x\\,\\,<\\,\\,4\n$$\n\nWe can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\in [2,4)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484224 :question/problem "Compute the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by the vectors $\\mathbf{p}=⟨1,2,3⟩$, $\\mathbf{q}=⟨0,5,1⟩$, and $\\mathbf{r}=⟨4,0,6⟩.$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The volume of a parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot (q\\times r) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{vmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(30)-2(0\\cdot 6-1\\cdot 4)+3(0\\cdot 0-5\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =30+8-60 \\\\  & =-22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is \n\n$$\nV=|-22|=22.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484229 :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{a}=⟨2,k,-1⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨-3,4,5⟩$, and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨6,k,1⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & k & -1 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 6 & k & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ k & 1\\end{vmatrix}-k\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 6 & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(4\\cdot 1-5\\cdot k)-k(-3\\cdot 1-5\\cdot 6)-1(-3\\cdot k-4\\cdot 6) \\\\  & =8-10k+33k+3k+24 \\\\  & =26k+32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =0 \\\\ 26k+32 & =0 \\\\ k & =-\\frac{16}{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-\\frac{16}{13}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484248 :question/problem "Calculate the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\mathbf{x}=⟨3,0,-2⟩$, $\\mathbf{y}=⟨1,2,4⟩$, and $\\mathbf{z}=⟨-5,1,1⟩.$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The volume of the parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (y\\times z) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(2\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 1)+0+(-2)(1\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-5)) \\\\  & =-6-22 \\\\  & =-28\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is \n\n$$\nV=|-28|=28.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485588 :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+z)$ and $\\mathbf{r}(t)=(t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+z))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+z))\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+z))\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+z)\\,i+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+z)\\,j+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+z)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t+1,$ $y=2t$ and $z={t}^{2}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})\\,i+(t+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})\\,j+(t+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =\\,i+2\\,j+2t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})\\,i+(t+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})\\,j+(t+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})\\,k)\\cdot (\\,i+2\\,j+2t\\,k) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})\\cdot 1+(t+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})\\cdot 2+(t+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})+2(t+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})+2t(t+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})+2(t+1)(1+t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})+2(t+1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488195 :question/problem "Which of the following regions is connected?" :question/worked-solution "A region $D$ is connected if every pair of points in $D$ can be joined by a smooth curve or a path in $D.$\n\nAmong the given options, the only one that satisfies this definition is the following:\n\n![](images/4d/4d7d916e62541ad8d41f6043c0d0e2f511f0b79d036f6fce6b4ce4500a357023.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488200 :question/problem "Which of the following regions is connected?" :question/worked-solution "A region $D$ is connected if every pair of points in $D$ can be joined by a smooth curve or a path in $D.$\n\nAmong the given options, the only one that satisfies this definition is the following:\n\n![](images/44/4496057807b8ae67cf1c8687a1118e0cbcb797726c7ecff1029f34397f521cfb.png)"}]}
